               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 32-year-old male, who presented on 30/01/2008 with a short history of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed localised peritonism with rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on intravenous antibiotics. Given the clinical picture, he underwent a laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 31/01/2008, which was uncomplicated. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Post-operatively, the patient recovered well, with wound care provided and vital signs remaining stable. He was discharged on 03/02/2008 in good condition, with advice regarding wound care and when to seek further medical attention. Please do not hesitate to contact me with any queries.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
